# Lab: File path traversal, validation of start of path

## Objective

The goal is to exploit the path traversal vulnerability and retrieve the contents of /etc/passwd.

## Solution

1. Open the lab and click on any product image.

2. Open Burp Suite > Proxy > HTTP history and find the request that loads the product image.

3. Send the request to Repeater.

4. Find the filename parameter.

5. The application validates that the supplied path starts with the expected directory.

6. Bypass this validation by keeping the expected directory at the beginning of the path and adding path traversal sequences afterward.

7. Change the filename parameter to:

/var/www/images/../../../etc/passwd

8. Send the request.

9. The response contains the contents of:

/etc/passwd

## Vulnerability Explanation

The application checks whether the supplied filename starts with the expected directory:

/var/www/images/

Because our payload starts with the expected directory, it passes the validation.

The application then resolves the path traversal sequences:

/var/www/images/../../../etc/passwd

The ../ sequences move up through the directories until reaching the root directory:

/var/www/images/
        ↓ ../
/var/www/
        ↓ ../
/var/
        ↓ ../
/
        ↓
/etc/passwd

Therefore, the application reads the /etc/passwd file instead of the intended product image.

## Conclusion

The path traversal vulnerability was successfully exploited by placing the expected directory at the beginning of the path and then using traversal sequences:

/var/www/images/../../../etc/passwd

The contents of /etc/passwd were successfully retrieved.

